"""Chapter 5 drill items — citizenship, rights, public administration, local government."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_5 = [
    q("Citizenship is best defined as", ["residence in a country", "legal membership of a state", "the right to vote", "the payment of taxes"], 1, "Legal membership, carrying rights and duties. Residence can last a lifetime without citizenship, the vote is one right of many, and tax is a duty rather than the status itself."),
    q("Which of the following is NOT a way of acquiring Nigerian citizenship?", ["By birth", "By descent", "By residence for five years", "By naturalisation"], 2, "Residence alone does not confer citizenship, and the naturalisation route requires fifteen years. Birth, descent and naturalisation are all routes in sections 25 to 27."),
    q("A person born outside Nigeria either of whose parents is a Nigerian citizen acquires citizenship by", ["birth", "descent", "registration", "naturalisation"], 1, "Descent. Birth as a route depends on being born in Nigeria to parents or grandparents of an indigenous community."),
    q("A foreign woman married to a Nigerian citizen may acquire Nigerian citizenship by", ["birth", "descent", "registration", "naturalisation"], 2, "Registration, under section 26. Naturalisation, under section 27, is the route for a resident of fifteen years who is not married to a citizen."),
    q("The period of residence required for citizenship by naturalisation in Nigeria is", ["five years", "ten years", "fifteen years", "twenty years"], 2, "Fifteen years, together with good character, a language of the community and acceptance by the local community."),
    q("Which of the following is a condition for citizenship by naturalisation?", ["Ownership of landed property", "Good character and acceptance by the community", "Membership of a political party", "Service in the armed forces"], 1, "Good character, residence, a language of the community and local acceptance are the statutory conditions. Property, party membership and military service are not required."),
    q("Nigerian law permits", ["no dual citizenship", "dual citizenship", "dual citizenship only for women", "dual citizenship only for persons born abroad"], 1, "Dual citizenship is permitted: a Nigerian who acquires another citizenship does not lose the Nigerian one, though a naturalised citizen may be required to renounce a former citizenship."),
    q("Which of the following is a duty of a citizen?", ["To vote in every election", "To pay taxes lawfully imposed", "To join a political party", "To serve as a minister"], 1, "Paying lawful taxes is a duty. Voting and party membership are rights that a citizen may exercise, and ministerial office is an appointment."),
    q("Fundamental human rights in Nigeria are contained in", ["Chapter I of the Constitution", "Chapter II of the Constitution", "Chapter IV of the Constitution", "the Electoral Act"], 2, "Chapter IV, sections 33 to 46. Chapter II holds the fundamental objectives, which are not enforceable in the courts."),
    q("Which of the following is a fundamental human right under the Nigerian Constitution?", ["Right to employment", "Right to free education", "Right to fair hearing", "Right to shelter"], 2, "Fair hearing is section 36, in Chapter IV. Employment, education and shelter are Chapter II objectives, and the Constitution says the courts will not enforce them."),
    q("A person whose fundamental right has been breached may apply for redress to", ["the President", "the National Assembly", "a High Court", "the police"], 2, "Section 46 gives the High Court jurisdiction to hear such an application. The other bodies neither adjudicate nor remedy a breach."),
    q("The right to life in Nigeria may lawfully be taken", ["at any time by the police", "in execution of a sentence of a court for a capital offence", "by a private citizen in self-defence only", "by the President's order"], 1, "Section 33 permits deprivation only in execution of a court sentence in respect of a criminal offence, and in a few other narrow cases such as defence of property."),
    q("The right to freedom of expression in Nigeria does not include the right to", ["hold opinions", "receive and impart information", "publish a newspaper", "incite violence or publish false information likely to cause a breach of the peace"], 3, "Section 39 protects expression, but the law may impose limits for defence, public safety, public order and morality. Incitement is outside the right."),
    q("The right to freedom of movement in Nigeria may be restricted", ["only by the Supreme Court", "in the interest of defence, public safety or public health", "never, under any circumstances", "by a private employer"], 1, "Section 41 permits restriction by law in the interests of defence, public safety, public order, public morality or public health — the standard limitation clause."),
    q("The National Human Rights Commission is best described as", ["a court of law", "an investigative and reporting body", "a department of the police", "a committee of the National Assembly"], 1, "It investigates complaints, visits places of detention and reports. It cannot make binding orders — that is a court's power."),
    q("The Public Complaints Commission is otherwise known as the", ["ombudsman", "auditor-general", "attorney-general", "chief whip"], 0, "It is Nigeria's ombudsman: it investigates administrative injustice by public bodies and recommends remedies."),
    q("Public administration is best defined as", ["the making of public policy", "the machinery by which government policy is carried out", "the adjudication of disputes", "the conduct of elections"], 1, "Implementation is the core: ministries, departments, agencies and officials carrying policy into effect. Making policy is the politician's function, and adjudication and elections are other institutions'."),
    q("Which of the following is a feature of the civil service?", ["Permanence", "Election by the people", "Profit-making", "Partisanship"], 0, "Permanence, with anonymity, impartiality and hierarchy. Election belongs to political office, profit to business, and partisanship is precisely what the service must not show."),
    q("The civil service is described as impartial because its officials", ["do not vote", "serve whichever government is in power", "cannot be dismissed", "are appointed by the courts"], 1, "They implement the policy of the government of the day, including policies they advised against. They vote like other citizens, can be dismissed for misconduct, and are appointed through the service commissions."),
    q("The body that appoints and disciplines federal civil servants in Nigeria is the", ["Federal Character Commission", "Federal Civil Service Commission", "National Assembly", "Judicial Service Commission"], 1, "The Federal Civil Service Commission, with parallel commissions for the states. The Federal Character Commission polices balance, and the Judicial Service Commission handles judicial officers."),
    q("Which of the following is a control on the civil service?", ["The Auditor-General's annual audit", "The army's approval of appointments", "A referendum", "The Chief Justice's approval of promotions"], 0, "The Auditor-General reports to the National Assembly on how money was spent. Legislative committees, the courts, the ombudsman and the Code of Conduct Bureau are the other controls."),
    q("The Code of Conduct Bureau requires public officers to", ["join a political party", "declare their assets", "serve for life", "live in the capital"], 1, "Asset declaration, to prevent conflict of interest and unexplained wealth. Party membership, tenure and residence are not its business."),
    q("A public corporation is best described as", ["a private company owned by shareholders", "a statutory body created to provide a service, with its own legal personality", "a department of a ministry", "a local government council"], 1, "It is created by statute, has its own legal personality and funds, and is run by a board. A ministry department has no separate personality, and a council is a tier of government."),
    q("Which of the following is a reason for establishing a public corporation?", ["To maximise profit for shareholders", "To provide essential services where private capital will not go", "To reduce the size of government", "To avoid accountability"], 1, "Service where the market will not provide it, natural monopoly, social objectives and strategic control are the reasons. Profit, small government and avoiding accountability are not."),
    q("A major problem of public corporations in Nigeria has been", ["excessive competition", "political interference and losses", "shortage of staff", "the absence of any board"], 1, "Political interference in management, overstaffing and losses covered by the treasury are the standard problems, and they are the case for privatisation."),
    q("Local government in Nigeria is guaranteed by", ["section 7 of the Constitution", "section 1 of the Constitution", "the Electoral Act", "a decree of 1999"], 0, "Section 7 guarantees a system of democratically elected local government councils, and the states are to ensure their existence under a law that provides for their powers."),
    q("The number of local government areas in Nigeria is", ["449", "589", "774", "801"], 2, "774. The uniform system dates from the 1976 reform, and the number has grown with state creation since."),
    q("Which of the following is a function of a local government council?", ["Defence of the country", "Collection of market tolls and rates", "Issuing currency", "Conducting federal elections"], 1, "Local revenue and local services — markets, roads, sanitation, health posts and primary education support. Defence, currency and federal elections are federal matters."),
    q("The main source of local government revenue in Nigeria is", ["loans from foreign banks", "statutory allocation from the Federation Account", "proceeds from the sale of crude oil", "grants from political parties"], 1, "Statutory allocation, supplemented by a share of state revenue and by internally generated revenue such as rates and tolls."),
    q("The 1976 local government reform is remembered for", ["creating the regions", "establishing a uniform system of local government councils", "abolishing local government", "creating the Federal Capital Territory"], 1, "It created a uniform system of councils across the country — the 301 that have since grown to 774. The FCT was created in the same period but by a separate decision."),
    q("A local government chairman in Nigeria is", ["appointed by the state governor", "elected by the people of the local government area", "appointed by the President", "chosen by the councillors alone"], 1, "Elected, together with the councillors, under section 7's requirement of a democratically elected council. State governors have sometimes appointed caretaker committees, which is the very practice the section is meant to exclude."),
    q("Which of the following is a Chapter II objective rather than a Chapter IV right?", ["Right to fair hearing", "Right to freedom of religion", "Right to adequate shelter", "Right to freedom of movement"], 2, "Adequate shelter is in Chapter II, section 16. The other three are Chapter IV rights enforceable under section 46."),
    q("The right to freedom from discrimination in Nigeria protects against differential treatment on grounds of", ["height and weight", "ethnic group, sex, religion and political opinion", "wealth only", "age only"], 1, "Section 42 lists ethnic group, place of origin, sex, religion, political opinion and circumstances of birth. The list is closed, and wealth and age are not on it."),
    q("Citizenship by honorary award is conferred by", ["the Senate", "the President", "the Chief Justice", "the state governor"], 1, "The President may confer citizenship on a person for exceptional service to the nation. It is the rarest route and does not require residence."),
    q("A citizen who acquires the citizenship of another country", ["automatically loses Nigerian citizenship", "does not lose Nigerian citizenship", "must renounce it within a year", "loses the right to vote only"], 1, "Dual citizenship is permitted, so the Nigerian citizenship is retained. A naturalised citizen may be asked to renounce a former citizenship, which is the reverse case."),
    q("Which of the following best explains why the civil service is permanent?", ["Its officials are elected", "It carries institutional memory and continuity across changes of government", "It is funded by foreign donors", "Its officials cannot retire"], 1, "Continuity is the purpose: a new minister inherits a service that knows the files. Officials do retire, and they are appointed rather than elected."),
    q("The doctrine of ministerial responsibility means that", ["the minister personally performs every duty of the ministry", "the minister answers to the legislature for the conduct of the ministry", "the minister cannot be dismissed", "the civil servants are responsible to the courts alone"], 1, "The minister answers in the House for the ministry's conduct, which is why anonymity for officials and responsibility for ministers go together."),
    q("Which of the following is NOT a source of local government revenue?", ["Market tolls", "Tenement rates", "Statutory allocation", "Company income tax"], 3, "Company income tax is collected by the Federal Inland Revenue Service and paid into the Federation Account; a council receives only its share of the allocation. Tolls, rates and the allocation are its own sources."),
    q("Which of the following is a merit of a public corporation over a government department?", ["It has a separate legal personality and can sue and be sued", "It is not accountable to anyone", "It cannot make a loss", "It is run by civil servants"], 0, "Legal personality and managerial flexibility are the merits. It remains accountable through its enabling Act and the ministry, can make losses, and is run by a board rather than directly by the civil service."),
    q("The fundamental objectives and directive principles in Chapter II are", ["enforceable in the courts", "a guide to state policy that the courts will not enforce directly", "superior to Chapter IV", "amendments to the Constitution"], 1, "Section 6(6)(c) excludes them from direct enforcement. They guide policy, and the courts have sometimes used them as an aid to interpretation, but they are not rights."),
]
